WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.120
Tech sector crash: −78%.

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:06.960
That is what one scenario did to a
portfolio of three invented chip stocks,

00:00:07.160 --> 00:00:09.540
$10,000 taken down to $2,200.

00:00:10.040 --> 00:00:12.900
Where does 78 come from?
Let me take it apart.

00:00:13.955 --> 00:00:17.175
Hi, this is Tanmay Kulkarni,
in for Humanitarians AI.

00:00:17.635 --> 00:00:19.155
This week I contributed to Mycroft,

00:00:19.275 --> 00:00:22.755
a book and a working repository of finance
pipelines, and it calls them recipes.

00:00:23.395 --> 00:00:25.315
One of them, the Scenario
Stress Testing Agent,

00:00:25.335 --> 00:00:28.355
shocks a portfolio with a named
scenario and reports what is left.

00:00:28.555 --> 00:00:30.395
The portfolios here are mine, invented.

00:00:30.675 --> 00:00:32.535
The tickers come from
the workflow's own list.

00:00:32.695 --> 00:00:36.095
The numbers are scenario arithmetic, not
predictions, and not investment advice.

00:00:37.211 --> 00:00:39.531
A scenario is a short
list of named shocks.

00:00:39.811 --> 00:00:41.331
Tech Sector Crash names five.

00:00:41.571 --> 00:00:44.831
Four are price shocks: market,
−15%; technology sector,

00:00:44.851 --> 00:00:46.971
−30; semiconductors, −35;

00:00:47.351 --> 00:00:48.951
and AI stocks, −40.

00:00:49.471 --> 00:00:50.951
The fifth is a volatility number.

00:00:51.111 --> 00:00:52.971
The engine's rule is simple:
for each holding,

00:00:52.991 --> 00:00:54.691
add up the shocks that belong to it.

00:00:54.751 --> 00:00:57.371
Hold a guess: if the
market alone falls 15%,

00:00:57.391 --> 00:00:59.131
how far does the biggest holding fall?

00:01:03.126 --> 00:01:05.066
Take the biggest holding, a chip designer.

00:01:05.406 --> 00:01:07.746
Everything belongs to the
market, so a block of −15.

00:01:08.026 --> 00:01:10.166
It is a semiconductor stock, so −35.

00:01:10.186 --> 00:01:12.726
And it is on the AI list, so −40.

00:01:13.046 --> 00:01:14.806
The engine adds them: −90.

00:01:15.046 --> 00:01:18.066
The workflow's own README prints
this exact sum as its worked example,

00:01:18.086 --> 00:01:20.506
which is a big part of why
it is so easy to check.

00:01:20.726 --> 00:01:24.346
It even names a different way to combine
them, so the rule is a choice you can see.

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:28.371
The third holding is a chip
maker that is not on the AI list.

00:01:28.571 --> 00:01:32.271
A block of −15 for the market,
−35 for semiconductors.

00:01:32.411 --> 00:01:34.071
That is −50, not 90.

00:01:34.431 --> 00:01:37.191
The second chip stock
takes −90, like the first.

00:01:37.271 --> 00:01:39.871
Now give each its share: 40%, 30%, 30%.

00:01:39.891 --> 00:01:44.191
36 + 27 + 15 makes 78,
and there is the number.

00:01:45.179 --> 00:01:47.459
A smaller scenario shows the same rule.

00:01:47.639 --> 00:01:50.339
An aggressive rate hike: market, −10%;

00:01:50.379 --> 00:01:53.339
growth stocks, −20;
technology sector, −15.

00:01:53.599 --> 00:01:55.179
Four growth names, equal shares.

00:01:55.259 --> 00:01:56.639
Three of them take −30.

00:01:56.979 --> 00:01:58.959
One is also filed under
the technology sector,

00:01:58.979 --> 00:02:01.539
so a third block lands
on it, and it takes −45.

00:02:02.219 --> 00:02:04.879
The book loses 33.75%,

00:02:04.899 --> 00:02:06.379
which the workflow labels critical,

00:02:06.619 --> 00:02:08.299
its word for losses above thirty.

00:02:09.224 --> 00:02:11.204
Here is the part I wanted to be sure of.

00:02:11.224 --> 00:02:13.324
To check the original,
I wrote a second adder,

00:02:13.384 --> 00:02:15.564
a Python copy of the
engine and its first steps,

00:02:15.584 --> 00:02:17.564
and ran both on the same portfolios.

00:02:18.184 --> 00:02:20.624
The original code comes straight
out of the workflow file.

00:02:20.824 --> 00:02:24.524
On 24 requests, every number,
every label and every message matched.

00:02:24.804 --> 00:02:28.084
Then I added 12 deliberate breaks to
my checks, and each one was caught.

00:02:29.067 --> 00:02:31.427
Before any adding,
a validator reads each portfolio,

00:02:31.447 --> 00:02:35.187
with the workflow's own rules and one
addition of mine, and it protects the sum.

00:02:35.907 --> 00:02:38.627
Portfolios whose shares total 0.6, or 1.2,

00:02:38.647 --> 00:02:41.187
are accepted, because the
workflow rescales them to one.

00:02:41.427 --> 00:02:43.707
A holding with no ticker,
a share that is not a number,

00:02:43.727 --> 00:02:46.427
an empty list: each is stopped,
with its reason written down.

00:02:46.687 --> 00:02:49.147
10 out of 10 of my
deliberately flawed portfolios.

00:02:49.427 --> 00:02:51.347
A sum of nothing never reaches the screen.

00:02:52.302 --> 00:02:55.542
Nothing here calls the outside
world. The call to a model,

00:02:55.622 --> 00:02:57.602
the market-data request and the reply to a

00:02:57.622 --> 00:03:00.082
caller are written down as
hand-offs, not approved.

00:03:00.522 --> 00:03:04.262
Five items in the recipe are still open,
and it says draft, on purpose.

00:03:04.542 --> 00:03:06.322
That leaves a good list for next time.

00:03:07.323 --> 00:03:10.663
So next time a stress test gives
you one number, ask what was added:

00:03:10.783 --> 00:03:14.363
which shocks, from which lists, and has
anyone run the adder beside the original?

00:03:14.783 --> 00:03:16.363
A good answer names the terms.

00:03:19.459 --> 00:03:22.619
This was Tanmay Kulkarni,
for Humanitarians AI.

00:03:23.139 --> 00:03:25.079
The contribution is pull request 74

00:03:25.379 --> 00:03:27.159
on the Mycroft repository.
